  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🎛️ Techno Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Cold, industrial names for aliases, collectives, labels and EPs."
    },
    "intro": "Good techno names are short, hard and a little anonymous: a single machine word, a real name, a code or a model number. This **techno name generator** makes artist aliases, duo and collective names, record labels and EP titles in that spirit, from <em>Ballast</em> to <em>Station Nord</em>.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good techno names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator, for artists, duos, labels and releases:</p><ul><li><strong>Module 101</strong>: a model-number alias, the way Detroit's pioneers named themselves.</li><li><strong>Vera Brenner</strong>: a plain real-sounding name, for a DJ who lets the music talk.</li><li><strong>NRTH</strong>: a word with the vowels taken out.</li><li><strong>Divider & Silo</strong>: two machine words for a duo.</li><li><strong>Saltworks Collective</strong>: an industrial building for a collective.</li><li><strong>Kessel Trax</strong>: a label named after a boiler, with the old Chicago \"Trax\".</li><li><strong>Spannung EP</strong>: a German word (\"tension\") for a Berlin-style release.</li><li><strong>Power Cut EP</strong>: an EP title that sounds like what happens at 4 a.m.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How techno names itself",
        "content": "<p>Techno began in Detroit in the mid-1980s, with Juan Atkins, Derrick May and Kevin Saunderson, often called the Belleville Three. Juan Atkins recorded as Model 500, which set a pattern: an alias that sounds like a machine. After the Berlin Wall came down, the music found a second home in Berlin, where the Tresor club opened in 1991 in the old vaults of a department store.</p><p>The generator follows both cities:</p><ul><li><strong>Artist aliases</strong> are machine words, codes or real names: single words like <em>Basalt</em> or <em>Traction</em>, codes like <em>Series 404</em>, names like <em>Tobias Vogel</em>.</li><li><strong>Duos and collectives</strong> sound like industrial units: <em>Grey Units</em>, <em>Foundry Sound System</em>.</li><li><strong>Record labels</strong> use \"Trax\", \"Audio\" or a factory word: <em>Anvil Trax</em>, <em>Ringway Electronics</em>. The \"Trax\" habit goes back to Chicago's house label Trax Records.</li><li><strong>EPs</strong> are often one German word or a catalogue-style number: <em>Rauch</em> (smoke), <em>Gleis 404</em> (track 404).</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Using the names",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Search Discogs, Beatport and SoundCloud</strong> before you commit. Short machine words are popular in techno, so a code or a two-word name is easier to own.</li><li><strong>Starting a label?</strong> Check the name isn't trademarked, and that the domain and social handles are free.</li></ul><p>For warmer dance music, try the <a href=\"house-music-name-generator.html\">House Music Name Generator</a>, or the <a href=\"drum-and-bass-name-generator.html\">Drum and Bass Name Generator</a> for something faster. For a DJ name in any style, there's the <a href=\"dj-name-generator.html\">DJ Name Generator</a>.</p><p>For more genres, see all the <a href=\"../categories/music_genres-names.html\">music genre name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What are good techno DJ names?",
        "answer": "Short, hard names that feel a little anonymous: a machine word (Basalt), a code (Module 101, Series 404) or a plain real name (Vera Brenner). Techno has favoured aliases since Juan Atkins recorded as Model 500."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I name a techno record label?",
        "answer": "Use an industrial word with Trax, Audio or Electronics: Anvil Trax, Kessel Trax, Ringway Electronics. Choose Record label for names built that way, then check the name and domain are free."
      },
      {
        "question": "Why are so many techno titles in German?",
        "answer": "Because Berlin became techno's second home after the Wall came down, with clubs like Tresor. German words like Kalt (cold) and Licht (light) suit the music's hard, minimal sound."
      }
    ]
  }
